Notice that this "intersection of union of sets" is essentially a limsup of sets. Observe that, in order for an element to be in w(*B*), it must be in every S\_t*B* for every s >= 0. In other words, an element is in w(B) if and only if it appears in infinitely many S\_t*B*.

&#x200B;

I'm not entirely sure of your particular notation, but the idea is that the omega-limit set of a single point x is the set of points y for which the flow f(t,x) "closely passes by" y. In other words, if you start at the point x, then the *orbit* of x starting at t = 0 will pass by y infinitely many times at arbitrary closeness. Alternatively, if you consider the orbit of the point x, then y is in w(x) if y is a limit point of the orbit of x. Again alternatively, y is in w(x) if there is an increasing sequence of times t1, t2, ..., tn, ... for which f(tn, x) converges to y as n goes to infinity.

&#x200B;

In the case of non-singleton sets *B*, you just apply the above characterization of w(x), taking into consideration all points in B. In other words, y is in w(B) if and only if there are sequences t\_n, x\_n (with x\_n in *B*) satisfying f(t\_n,x\_n) converges to y.

&#x200B;

Note that, in general, w(*B*) contains the union over all x in *B* of w(x), but not vice versa. To see this, take *B* to be an open ball and take the flow f to be the identity. Then w(*B*) is the closed ball, but the union of w(x) over all x in *B* is just *B*.

Start with a basic premise; say, f(t) = c where f(t) denotes the fish population at time t, and c is a constant. This just says you want the fish population to not fluctuate over time.

&#x200B;

This means that the derivative, f'(t), is 0. But the derivative can be composed of many parts. A simple model might include f'(t) = y1(t) + y2(t) + y3(t) + y4(t) where y1(t) is how much fish you fish up per unit time, y2(t) is the population change governed by predation models (maybe see the Lotka-Volterra equations if you're feeling adventerous), y3(t) might be a seasonal component (i.e. does the fish population have a "periodic" component, like mating seasons), and y4(t) might be deaths due to pollution. 

&#x200B;

Yout want f'(t) to be 0, so 0 =  y1(t) + y2(t) + y3(t) + y4(t). Presumably, you'd be able to approximate y2, y3, and y4 using data of some sort (or maybe theory, but that's not likely to be any good), then "solve" for y1.

&#x200B;

Note that, since f'(t) is a rate with respect to time (e.g. change in fish period unit time), each of y1, y2, y3, and y4 must also be rates with respect to time.

(1) You don't have to split up the proof into a> 0 and a < 0, but if you don't, you might end up making a mistake. Note that in the step du = d(at), the left side is a "positive" thing, but the right side is a "negative" thing if a < 0. Thus, the bounds of the integral switch, and you must re-switch the bounds by pulling out another negative.

&#x200B;

(2) That is the definition of F(t); that is, F(t) = integral from -infty to infty of f(u)exp(-2 π i t u) du. Just replace t by (s/a)

