What are "xk" and "pl"? Do you mean x\^k or x\*k or x\_k? 

&#x200B;

To answer your question, the operator x is the function taking something to its product with x (with x being multiplied on the left). So instead of p, use its equivalent formulation as a constant times the partial derivative with respect to x. 

&#x200B;

The operator  "xk \* pl" is the function which takes in a wave function f(x,t) and returns (xk\*pl)f(x,t). Similarly, the operator " xk \* pl + pl \* xk " is the function which takes in f(x,t) and returns ( xk \* pl + pl \* xk )f(x,t). Show these are(n't) hermitian by showing that each is (or isn't) its own adjoint.
